`aplan verify` opens the repository (unlike hermetic `check`). Its resolution
behaviour, APLAN011/012/015 against real commits, is covered exhaustively by
the git-backed corpus in `crates/aplan-e2e`; here we pin the CLI contract.

A bad `--format` is rejected before anything else, so it needs no repository:

```
$ aplan verify --format bogus
? 2
aplan: `aplan verify` renders a set of findings, so it takes --format `human`, `json`, `agent`, `github` or `sarif`, not `bogus`

```

Applying repairs writes task files, so a machine report cannot be mixed with
mutation narration:

```
$ aplan verify --fix --format json
? 2
aplan: `aplan verify --fix` writes task files and supports only `--format human`; run `aplan verify --format json` to review the typed repairs without applying them

```
